# What may enter a build context, written as a deny-everything list with named
# exceptions rather than a list of things to keep out.
#
# There was no .dockerignore here until 2026-08-21, so the context for both
# images was the whole working tree: `.git` with every branch and every commit,
# `.venv`, `frontend/node_modules`, the pytest and mypy caches, and `data/`.
#
# `data/` is the one that decides the shape of this file. It holds the NEDU
# standard profile on a developer machine, which is somebody else's data whose
# redistribution terms are not confirmed, and both images are published to a
# public registry. A deny-list would have kept it out only for as long as
# somebody remembered to name every new place a copy could appear. An
# allow-list keeps it out by default, and keeps out the next such file too.
#
# The cost of an allow-list is that a Dockerfile which starts copying something
# new fails at build time with "file not found" until that path is named here.
# That is the failure mode to want: loud, immediate, and in the build rather
# than in the registry. tests/test_stack_smoke.py reads every COPY source out
# of both Dockerfiles and fails if one of them is not named below, so the
# breakage is a red test before it is a red build.
*

# --- infra/api.Dockerfile ---------------------------------------------------
# The lockfile and the manifest, resolved in the build stage.
!pyproject.toml
!uv.lock
# The two pure packages and the Django project.
!ampeer_sim
!ampeer_advice
!backend
!infra/entrypoint-api.sh

# --- infra/web.Dockerfile ---------------------------------------------------
!infra/nginx/nginx.conf
# The statically exported site, exactly as `frontend-test` verified it. It is
# git-ignored and built before the image; an empty or absent `out/` is caught
# by the four assertions inside web.Dockerfile, not here.
!frontend/out

# --- taken back out of the directories re-included above --------------------
# Compiled Python from the host. It is architecture and interpreter specific,
# it is never what `uv sync` produced inside the image, and a stale .pyc that
# shadows a source file is a bug that only exists in the image.
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
# A local .env anywhere in the tree. `*` already excludes every one of them and
# no exception above re-includes one, so this line adds nothing today. It is
# here because `infra/.env` is exactly where this project tells an operator to
# put the signing key and the database password, and the `!infra/...` lines
# above make infra/ read as though it were included. A future `!infra` would
# otherwise sweep that file into a public image in one character.
**/.env
**/.env.*
